Query Optimization Basics

.Poorly written queries:
    .make DB slow
    .use too many resources
    .cause locking problems
    .give users bad experience 



.Best Practices:

1. Use indexes wisely 
    .indexes help DB find data faster 
        .primary index - automatically created on the primary key; keeps values unique and fast to access
        .secondary index - created manually on non-primary key columns to improve query performance
        .clustered index - determines the arrangement of data in a table
                    .in some tables, primary key is automatically indexed 
        .non-clustered index - contains pointers to the data 
                    .multiple exist per table 

        
    Indexing guidelines:
        .index columns used often in WHERE, JOIN, or ORDER BY clauses 
        .avoid too many indexes - they slow down INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE
        .check and monitor index usage regularly to keep queries fast 


2. Avoid select *: Choose only required columns
    .makes queries slow, esp for large tables or when joining multiple tables 
    .avoiding will:
        .use less memory and run faster
        .let DB skip unneeded columns 
        .make queries simpler and easier to read 


3. Limit rows with where and limit 
